    "result_text": "The full suite passes (`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, plus the `AcceptanceBase`/`AuditLogCapture` support classes). Build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization applied whenever an owner is created, keeping each concern in its own small unit and reusing the existing normalizer/validator patterns.\n\n**New component \u2014 `AddressNormalizer`** (`rest/controller/AddressNormalizer.java`)\nMirrors the existing `EmailNormalizer`/`TelephoneNormalizer` style. Its single `normalize(String)`:\n- trims and collapses internal whitespace,\n- upper-cases,\n- expands street-type abbreviations token-by-token (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`),\n- returns `\"\"` for a null/blank input.\n\n**`OwnerFieldsValidator`** \u2014 injected the normalizer and normalize the address into the DTO at the start of `validate(...)`, *before* the required-field loop. Because the write-back happens first, the existing `rejectIfEmptyOrWhitespace` check now rejects an address that is blank after normalization, and the mapper stores/returns the canonical value \u2014 the same mechanism already used for telephone/email.\n\n**`HouseholdMatcher`** \u2014 injected the normalizer and use it for the address component of the household key (renaming its own lenient helper to `normalizeName`, still used for last name). This makes both address comparisons \u2014 duplicate detection and the shared household id \u2014 use the canonical normalized form, rather than the matcher's weaker case/whitespace-only normalization that didn't expand abbreviations. `HouseholdDuplicateChecker` and `HouseholdAssigner` inherit this automatically since they route through the matcher.\n\nResults:\n- `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`\n- `\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`\n- `\"   \"` \u2192 rejected with 400\n\nAll 3 new acceptance tests and the full suite pass.",
